Well, and that pretty much defines what is going
wrong. You are taking actions based on superstition,
based on "let's bang on it and see what happens",
based on "everything should work as I expect it to
work". None of those are useful attitudes in getting
computers to do what you want them to do.

When you don't realize that compilers and build
scripts are tools created by humans; that those
humans may very well not have the same attitudes as
you do for what "reasonable behavior" from those
compilers and build scripts is, as you do; that
those humans sometimes make mistakes themselves;
that directions for using compilers and build
scripts assume a basic level of computer skills that
you seem to lack; then the biggest impediment to
your progress and success is not the computer, the
software you're trying to compile, the compiler, the
scripts being used to control the compiler, or the
directions. The biggest impediment is you.

Now here's a very labor intensive way to do what you
are trying to do, that will both help you learn, and
possibly get you to your goal.

Find the makefile.

Line by line, cut and paste from the makefile to a
single line script, doing all the substitutions of
tunable parameters on that single line, _by hand_.

Of necessity, this means you learn how to read and
write a makefile.

In some cases, a "line" may be paramaterized to
execute over the whole set of source files. If so,
do them one by one.

Look at the line until you understand what it is
trying to accomplish.

Execute the line by running the single line script.

Look at the results. Did the computer produce the
output you expected it to produce? Is there anything
in the object produced (as opposed to it being an
empty file)? If so, do the next line from the
makefile.

If not, figure out what went wrong. Did you
misunderstand the purpose of the line? Did you
mispatch the parameters? Was some component the line
needed to execute correctly missing? Did the
intended action produce some error message? Did the
intended action fail silently?

When you get to the end of this program, you should
be able to understand _in detail_ how
NetHack/SlashEm is constructed, how the tools you
are using work, and so forth.

The one advantage you have in following this
approach is that NetHack/SlashEm is composed of
_huge_ source code and header code files, so there
aren't really too many things that have to be done,
at a number of chunks level. Were the code properly
modularized, there would probably be twenty-fold
more "things to do" than there are in
NetHack/SlashEm as it is.[*]

The biggest clue you need to grasp, one you've had
thrown at you half a dozen times and failed each
time to catch, is that when using a computer, things
happen, go right, or go wrong, for a _reason_, and
your task is finding the reasons for the stuff that
goes wrong, and fixing them, _not_ whining "but I
didn't see any error messages, so magic should have
happened".

